      "id": 1,
      "text": "Casa Blanca was founded in 1754 by Captain Tomás Sánchez de la Barrera y Gallardo, who built the iconic White House on Peñitas Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casa_Blanca%2C_Texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The White House was constructed of caliche blocks called ciares, giving the settlement its name that means “white house” in Spanish.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Casa_Blanca%2C_Texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1894 a sudden evacuation forced residents to abandon Casa Blanca, ending nearly a century of continuous habitation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/casa-blanca-texas-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Economic hardship, droughts, and shifting trade routes pushed the community toward abandonment, leaving rich archaeological remains today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/casa-blanca-texas-ghost-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The settlement once served as a ranching hub, with the property operated as a ranch until 1973 before its final desertion.",
